00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.320
Our master of microscopes, James, doesn’t&nbsp;
live particularly close to the sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.840
But as we’ve seen in some previous episodes, that&nbsp;
distance has not stopped him from finding ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.640
explore marine life. If he can’t easily get to&nbsp;
the sea, well, the sea can be brought to him in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:01.360
monthly deliveries of sand and sea water. So among&nbsp;
all the other microbe collections in his home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:07.840
James has fish tanks with no fish—just&nbsp;
archives of those marine deliveries.&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.200
And some of those tanks have been around&nbsp;
for a while, so you might think that he’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.560
found everything there was to find in&nbsp;
them. But the sea is always delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.760
new surprises, even when it’s been firmly&nbsp;
encased in a fish tank for more than a year.&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.600
Recently, one of these old tanks turned&nbsp;
up some surprising tentacled creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.240
At first glance, James thought they might&nbsp;
be a Journey to the Microcosmos favorite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:39.760
the hydra…except hydra don’t particularly care&nbsp;
for saltwater. They are freshwater creatures.&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.720
So naturally, James decided to take a closer look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.760
studying his miniature&nbsp;
creatures under the microscope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.720
He could see rows of tentacles attached to a pod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.360
So with this in mind, James began&nbsp;
digging through his books on Cnidaria—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.040
phylum that hydras belong to—and he narrowed&nbsp;
in on a possible species: the Moerisia.&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.360
That name may not mean anything to&nbsp;
you right now. But when James saw it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:21.840
it made him realize something else: somewhere&nbsp;
in his tank, there must be a jellyfish.&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.640
Now to understand why James had this realization,&nbsp;
you need to first hear a little bit more about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:36.080
the moerisia actually lives. An important part of&nbsp;
that is that the moerisia actually has two lives.&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.760
Moerisia are a type of hydrozoan, a group of&nbsp;
cnidarians that includes about 3700 species.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.720
While they’re not as well-studied as other famous&nbsp;
hydrozoans like the hydra or the Portuguese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.560
man-o-war, we do know that several Moerisia&nbsp;
species likely originated in the Black Sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.760
but have since spread widely—even found&nbsp;
in estuaries in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.600
Their spread has probably been helped by&nbsp;
the fact that they’re able to withstand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.520
a wide range of temperatures and salinities,&nbsp;
making it easy for them to navigate new waters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.680
They survive on zooplankton like our&nbsp;
favorite crustacean, the copepod.&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.440
And as we said earlier, the Moerisia has two&nbsp;
lives. One, as we’ve been observing, is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:29.120
polyp form. And the other, is this…the medusa.&nbsp;
Or as James named it: Squishy the Jellyfish.&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.360
The double life is a common hydrozoan trait,&nbsp;
though we should note that Hydras are a notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:42.560
exception, living strictly in a polyp form.
But for hydrozoans like the moerisia, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.920
are a little more complex. When they hatch, they&nbsp;
emerge from an egg as a larva called a planula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.440
which settles onto the ground and develops&nbsp;
into a single polyp, radially symmetric and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.520
usually attached to objects like plants, shells,&nbsp;
or rocks. And from there, the polyps begin to bud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.840
forming more and more polyps until there’s a&nbsp;
whole colony of them attached to each other.&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.640
Within this colony, polyps can take on different&nbsp;
forms to serve different functions. Some might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.040
take on spiny tentacles that allow them&nbsp;
to defend the colony, while others have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.960
mouths dedicated to eating. Still others might&nbsp;
be dedicated strictly to reproductive function.&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:27.600
The polyp stage is clearly distinct from the&nbsp;
medusa stage. One looks more like a diffuse tree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.760
while the other looks more like a bag with&nbsp;
strings dangling off of it. But the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.560
crux of their difference is not in their&nbsp;
appearance, but in their reproduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.320
Because hydrozoans can reproduce in both&nbsp;
stages, just very differently. As a polyp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.800
it reproduces asexually, budding off to form&nbsp;
new polyps and eventually to form the medusa.&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:55.440
You can see the polyp actually budding off to&nbsp;
create this next stage of life, that little nub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.760
eventually expanding and releasing&nbsp;
to create what will end up resembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:06.080
an inverted polyp…or an umbrella. And&nbsp;
unlike the sessile, anchored polyp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.320
medusas are free-swimming. Inside its opening&nbsp;
is an inner ring of tissue called the velum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.280
which helps the medusa move&nbsp;
around by tensing up and relaxing.&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:23.440
For this Moerisia species, the medusa are&nbsp;
phototaxic, meaning they like swimming toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.320
light. That’s actually how James found them&nbsp;
in his tank. After identifying the possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.040
species and reading that they like to swim toward&nbsp;
light, he decided to check the side of the tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.560
that received all the morning sunshine.&nbsp;
When he did, he found his tiny jellies.&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.600
But the main function of this form is&nbsp;
less about light and more about sex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.120
In Moerisia that have been raised in the lab,&nbsp;
the medusa takes about 8 days to develop gonads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.360
And when they do, they will&nbsp;
proceed to their next step: sex.&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.960
The exact details of how hydrozoans&nbsp;
have sex varies with different species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.160
and we haven’t been able to find an answer to&nbsp;
exactly how the Moerisia do it in the literature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.000
But we want to briefly state that&nbsp;
one of the hydrozoan possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.560
is that the male releases sperm into&nbsp;
the water, and the female releases eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.920
that chemically attract the sperm to them.&nbsp;
The fertilization is entirely external,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.120
just happening in the water, and eventually that&nbsp;
fertilized egg will develop into a new organism.&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:37.280
Here’s an important question though, why the&nbsp;
double life? Why two different stages that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:44.800
each reproduce in completely different ways? Well,&nbsp;
because asexual and sexual reproduction are both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.680
pretty nifty talents to have on hand. Asexual&nbsp;
reproduction is fast, and it helps polyps build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:57.760
their local populations quickly. The downside&nbsp;
is that an asexual polyp means limiting both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:03.360
scenery and the gene pool. With that comes the&nbsp;
risk that the environment will shift suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.040
and both the polyp and the entire population&nbsp;
will be unable to adapt fast enough to survive.&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.920
But by leading a second sexually reproductive&nbsp;
life as a medusa, the hydrozoan gets to disperse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.080
both physically and genetically. But there is a&nbsp;
cost—after all, sexual reproduction takes time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.640
and special organs and the hope that sperm will&nbsp;
meet an egg. But that is why the hydrozoan doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:35.360
strictly rely on sexual reproduction either.
This combined strategy is part of why the Moerisia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:42.560
has been so successful at traveling beyond&nbsp;
its origins. It gets the best of both worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.840
as an organism that is at times static&nbsp;
and dividing, and at other times swimming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:58.160
and mixing. Each generation produces a large local&nbsp;
population that can eventually expand and spread.&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.920
We should, here at the end however, admit one&nbsp;
thing. Technically, our Squishy the Jellyfish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:11.040
is not a “true” jellyfish. To be considered a&nbsp;
true jellyfish, an animal has to be a member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:17.840
of the class Scyphozoa. And Scyphozoa—like the&nbsp;
hydrozoa—are part of the Cnidarian phylum. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:24.000
while Moerisia and their fellow medusa hydrozoa&nbsp;
do bear a strong resemblance and relationship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.840
their true jelly relatives, they also have their&nbsp;
own unique structures that make them distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.560
(and, alas, not true jellyfish).
But in our tank of sea water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:41.280
Squishy the Jellyfish will keep his title. He&nbsp;
has worked and traveled a long way to earn it.&nbsp;

